course: books
lesson: 12
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter VI, part 2: The baby, the pig and the Cheshire Cat
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The Duchess flings the baby at Alice and rushes off to get ready for croquet with the Queen; Alice carries the squirming, star-fish-shaped baby outside, afraid that in that house they would kill it.
explain: But the baby keeps grunting, its nose turns into a snout and its eyes get tiny, until it is quite plainly a pig, so Alice puts it down and it trots off happily into the wood.
explain: The Cheshire Cat is grinning on a branch; Alice asks it which way to go, and it says that depends on where she wants to get to, then tells her that everyone round here is mad, the Hatter and the March Hare, the Cat and Alice too.
explain: The Cat keeps vanishing and appearing, and at last fades away slowly, starting with its tail and ending with its grin, which hangs in the air on its own.
explain: Alice heads for the March Hare's house, with ear-shaped chimneys and a fur roof, makes herself two feet tall and walks up nervously: a strange, dreamy part, both funny and a bit creepy.
fact: The Duchess says "Here! you may nurse it a bit, if you like!", flinging the baby at Alice, and goes to get ready to play croquet with the Queen; the cook throws a frying-pan after her but misses.
fact: The baby holds out its arms and legs "just like a star-fish", is "snorting like a steam-engine", and keeps doubling up and straightening out.
fact: The "proper way of nursing it" is to twist it into a sort of knot and hold tight to its right ear and left foot.
fact: Alice thinks "If I don't take this child away with me, they're sure to kill it in a day or two: wouldn't it be murder to leave it behind?"
fact: The baby grunts, and Alice tells it "Don't grunt, that's not at all a proper way of expressing yourself."
fact: It has "a very turn-up nose, much more like a snout than a real nose", and very small eyes, and at last it is "neither more nor less than a pig".
fact: Alice sets the pig down and feels "quite relieved" to see it trot away into the wood; she says "If it had grown up, it would have made a dreadfully ugly child: but it makes rather a handsome pig, I think."
fact: The Cheshire Cat is sitting on a bough (a branch) of a tree; it looks good-natured but has "very long claws and a great many teeth", so Alice thinks it "ought to be treated with respect", and calls it "Cheshire Puss".
fact: Alice asks "Would you tell me, please, which way I ought to go from here?", and the Cat answers "That depends a good deal on where you want to get to."; Alice says "I don't much care where—so long as I get somewhere", and the Cat says "Then it doesn't matter which way you go."
fact: The Cat says a Hatter lives one way and a March Hare the other: "Visit either you like: they're both mad", and "we're all mad here"; Alice must be mad too, it says, "or you wouldn't have come here"; "mad as a hatter" and "mad as a March hare" were old sayings, because hares leap and box wildly in spring.
fact: "Mad" in this book means crazy and silly; calling real people "mad" because they are ill is unkind.
fact: The Cat's proof: a dog "growls when it's angry, and wags its tail when it's pleased", but "I growl when I'm pleased, and wag my tail when I'm angry. Therefore I'm mad." Alice says "I call it purring, not growling."
fact: The Cat asks if Alice plays croquet with the Queen today; she has not been invited yet, and the Cat says "You'll see me there", and vanishes (that is the first time); it appears again to ask "what became of the baby?", and when Alice says "It turned into a pig" it says "I thought it would" and vanishes again (the second time); Alice is not much surprised, "she was getting so used to queer things happening".
fact: Alice thinks that "as this is May" the March Hare "won't be raving mad—at least not so mad as it was in March".
fact: The Cat asks "Did you say pig, or fig?", then vanishes slowly, "beginning with the end of the tail, and ending with the grin"; before that Alice asks it not to "keep appearing and vanishing so suddenly: you make one quite giddy", and afterwards thinks "I've often seen a cat without a grin, but a grin without a cat!"
fact: The March Hare's house is so large that Alice does not like to go nearer till she has nibbled some more of the left-hand bit and grown to about two feet high.
fact: Old words in today's English: "by-the-bye" = by the way, "giddy" = dizzy, "you grant that?" = do you agree?, "thatched" = roofed (usually with straw, here with fur).
exercise: Why did the Duchess hurry out of the kitchen? | To get ready to play croquet with the Queen | To fetch more pepper for the soup | To find a doctor for the sneezing baby | To chase the Frog-Footman away from her front door
exercise: What did the cook throw after the Duchess? | A frying-pan | A kettle | A plate | A three-legged stool
exercise: What did Alice think the baby looked like, with its arms and legs stuck out? | A star-fish | A spider | A windmill | A crab on the beach
exercise: What did the baby sound like when Alice caught it? | A steam-engine | A tiny bird | A fire alarm | A kitten mewing for milk
exercise: What was "the proper way of nursing" this baby? | Twist it into a knot and hold its right ear and left foot | Rock it gently in her arms and sing it a lullaby | Lay it in a basket and push it about in the wood | Wrap it in a warm blanket and pat it on the back
exercise: Why did Alice take the baby out of the house? | She feared they would kill it in a day or two | The Duchess told her to take it to the Queen | It wanted to go out and play in the garden | The cook asked her to take it far away into the wood
exercise: What did Alice tell the baby when it grunted? | "Don't grunt" | "Don't cry" | "Don't sneeze" | "Don't bite"
exercise: What was strange about the baby's nose? | It was turned up, much more like a snout | It was very long, like the Pigeon's beak | It was blue, like the Caterpillar's skin | It kept vanishing and appearing again
exercise: What did the baby turn into? | A pig | A frog | A cat | A puppy
exercise: What did Alice do with the pig? | Set it down and let it trot into the wood | Gave it back to the Duchess in the kitchen | Took it home to show her big sister | Put it in her pocket with the thimble
exercise: What did Alice say about the pig? | It makes rather a handsome pig | It was the ugliest pig she had seen | It ought to go back to the Duchess | It would make a dreadful pet for her
exercise: Where was the Cheshire Cat sitting? | On a bough of a tree | On top of the mushroom | On the kitchen hearth | On the March Hare's roof
exercise: Why did Alice think the Cat should be treated with respect? | It had very long claws and a great many teeth | It belonged to the Queen of the whole country | It was the oldest creature in all the wood | It knew the way to the lovely garden
exercise: What did Alice call the Cat? | "Cheshire Puss" | "Dinah dear" | "O Cat" | "Sir Cat, if you please"
exercise: What did Alice ask the Cat first? | Which way she ought to go from here | Why it grinned so very widely | Where the White Rabbit had gone | How to get back up the rabbit-hole
exercise: What did the Cat say the way depends on? | Where she wants to get to | How tall she is | What time it is | Who she really is inside
exercise: Who lives in the two directions the Cat waves its paws at? | A Hatter and a March Hare | The Duchess and the Queen | The Rabbit and the Mouse | The Dodo and the Lory
exercise: What does the Cat say they both are? | Mad | Asleep | Late | Rich
exercise: Why, says the Cat, must Alice be mad? | Or she wouldn't have come here | Because she talks to herself | Because she changes her size | Because she ate the mushroom from the Caterpillar
exercise: What does a dog do when it is pleased, according to the Cat? | Wags its tail | Growls | Purrs | Grins from ear to ear
exercise: What did Alice call the Cat's "growling"? | Purring | Singing | Snoring | Hissing
exercise: Where did the Cat say Alice would see it? | At the Queen's croquet game | At the Hatter's tea-table | At the Rabbit's little house | Back at the Duchess's kitchen
exercise: What did the Cat ask when it came back the first time? | What became of the baby | Where the Duchess had gone | Whether Alice liked cats | Which way the Queen went
exercise: What did the Cat say when Alice told it the baby turned into a pig? | "I thought it would" | "How very strange!" | "Off with its head!" | "Call it what you like"
exercise: Here "giddy" means... | Dizzy | Happy | Sleepy | Silly
exercise: How did the Cat vanish the last time? | Slowly, tail first and grin last | All at once, in a puff of smoke | Slowly, grin first and tail last | Quickly, running off into the trees
exercise: How did Alice know the March Hare's house? | The chimneys were shaped like ears and the roof was thatched with fur | It had a brass plate on the door that said "M. HARE" in big letters | A footman with a face like a fish stood guarding the front door | The Cheshire Cat was sitting on its roof grinning down at her
exercise: How tall did Alice make herself before going near the March Hare's house? | About two feet | About nine inches | About four feet | About three inches
exercise: Thinking back to part 1: what word did the Duchess shout at the baby, which turned out to fit? | "Pig!" | "Serpent!" | "Goose!" | "Mabel!"
exercise: Thinking back to chapter V: which bit of mushroom made Alice grow taller? | The left-hand bit | The right-hand bit | The stalk | The top
test: Freddie goes: "Alice dropped the baby 'cos it was too heavy." Put him right. | She set it down because it had turned into a pig | She dropped it because it bit her on the hand | She gave it to the Cat to look after for her | She dropped it when the cook threw a pan at her
test: Maya, Gen Alpha: "The Cat said 'we're all mad here', it's giving villain era." ("it's giving villain era" = it looks like a baddie.) How did the Cat prove Alice was mad? | "You must be, or you wouldn't have come here" | "You growl when you're pleased, like me" | "You talk to yourself all the time" | "You have a very long neck"
test: Why is the Cat's proof that it is mad not a good proof? | A cat's purr is not the same as a dog's growl | Cats really do growl when they are angry | Dogs wag their tails when they are angry | The Cat never said anything about dogs
test: What would have happened, Alice thought, if she had left the baby in the kitchen? | They would have killed it in a day or two | The Duchess would have sung it to sleep | The Cat would have looked after it | It would have turned into a cat
test: Grandad from Yorkshire: "Nay, a baby turnin' into a pig, that's daft as a brush." ("daft as a brush" = very silly.) Which clue came first that it was turning into a pig? | Its grunting | Its pink tail | Its four hooves | Its curly ears
test: True or false: Alice is upset when the baby turns into a pig. | False: she feels "quite relieved" to see it trot away | True: she cries and runs after it into the wood | True: she takes it back to the Duchess at once | False: she laughs and puts it in her pocket
test: Kayla, rough: "Bloody cat won't give a straight answer, does my head in." ("does my head in" = drives me crazy.) Which answer is NOT straight? | "That depends a good deal on where you want to get to" | "In that direction lives a Hatter" | "It turned into a pig," said Alice | "I should like it very much," said Alice
test: Why does Alice think the March Hare might be less mad now? | Because it is May, not March | Because it is a very hot day | Because the Cat said so | Because the hare is old now
test: Why is "a grin without a cat" so curious? | A grin is part of a face, so it can't stay without the face | Cats are never seen grinning in real life at all | The grin was much bigger than the cat had been | The grin kept talking after the cat had gone
test: Oisin from Galway: "Sure the Cat was grand, but it kept poppin' in and out like an eejit." What did Alice ask it to stop doing? | Appearing and vanishing so suddenly | Grinning so widely at her | Talking about the Queen | Asking her about the baby
test: Looking back: in chapter IV Alice made a plan to get to her right size and then to the garden. What does she tell the Cat about where she wants to go? | That she doesn't much care where, so long as she gets somewhere | That she must get straight to the lovely garden behind the door | That she wants to go home to Dinah and her sister on the bank | That she wants to visit the Queen and play croquet with her
test: Looking back: Alice has met two cats in her mind and in Wonderland. How is Dinah different from the Cheshire Cat? | Dinah stays at home; the Cheshire Cat vanishes and talks | Dinah grins and the Cheshire Cat catches mice | Both of them vanish whenever they like | Dinah lives in the Duchess's kitchen
test: Looking back to chapter V: Alice made herself nine inches to visit the Duchess's little house. Why does she grow to two feet before the March Hare's house? | The house is so large that she wants to be bigger first | She wants to be small enough to get in at the window | The Cat told her to be exactly two feet tall | The March Hare only lets small people inside
test: How has Alice changed since chapter I, when she wondered at the White Rabbit's watch? | Strange things hardly surprise her now | She is more surprised by everything now | She has stopped talking to animals | She is now afraid of all cats
test: ★★★ Count the Cat's vanishings in this part: after "You'll see me there", after "I thought it would", and the slow one at the end. How many times did it vanish? | Three | Two | Four | Only once
test: ★★★ "Did you say pig, or fig?" Why might the Cat mix them up? | They sound almost the same, with only the first sound different | Pigs and figs are both found in the Duchess's kitchen | The baby had been eating figs before it became a pig | The Cat cannot hear well because of all the pepper
test: ★★★ Alice says "I don't much care where—", and the Cat answers "Then it doesn't matter which way you go." Why is the Cat right? | If you have no place to reach, any road will do | Every road in Wonderland leads to the same garden | The Cat is wrong; one road is always the best | Alice had already chosen to visit the Hatter
exam: Identify the transformation undergone by the infant entrusted to the protagonist. | It became a pig | It became a cat | It became a frog | It became a serpent
exam: Ascertain the reply of the Cheshire Cat to the protagonist's enquiry as to which way she ought to go. | That it depended upon where she wished to arrive | That she ought to proceed directly to the Queen | That she should return forthwith to the kitchen | That every direction led to the lovely garden
exam: It is given that the Cat claimed to growl when pleased. Determine the term which the protagonist preferred for this sound. | Purring | Snoring | Mewing | Hissing
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the order in which the Cat finally vanished. | Commencing with the tail and concluding with the grin | Commencing with the grin and concluding with the tail | Vanishing in its entirety in a single instant | Commencing with the ears and concluding with the paws
